  "problem": "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-10{x}^{2}+8$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}\u2248$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{\u2032}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{\u2032}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=({x}^{4}-10{x}^{2}+8)^{\u2032}=4{x}^{3}-20x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{4}-10{x}_{n}^{2}+8}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n})-({x}_{n}^{4}-10{x}_{n}^{2}+8)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{4}-10{x}_{n}^{2}-8}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{4}-10{x}_{0}^{2}-8}{4{x}_{0}^{3}-20{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(0.5)^{4}-10(0.5)^{2}-8}{4(0.5)^{3}-20(0.5)} \\\\  & =1.085\\,526\u2026\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{4}-10{x}_{n}^{2}-8}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1.085\\,526\u2026)^{4}-10(1.085\\,526\u2026)^{2}-8}{4(1.085\\,526\u2026)^{3}-20(1.085\\,526\u2026)} \\\\  & \u22480.941,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.",
